"""English: extended material for Stage 1 · Statistics basics."""
from . import LE

LE("dasar-jenis-data",
   [("Population, sample, parameter, and statistic",
     [r"A population is everything we want to draw conclusions about, such as all rice fields in a district. A sample is the part of the population that is actually observed. A quantity describing the population is a parameter and is usually written with a Greek letter (\(\mu\), \(\sigma\), \(\pi\)); a quantity computed from the sample is a statistic and is written with a Latin letter (\(\bar{x}\), \(s\), \(\hat{p}\)).",
      r"Inferential statistics works in two directions: from the sample we estimate the parameter (estimation), then judge how certain that estimate is (confidence intervals and hypothesis tests). Because different samples give different statistics, every statistic has its own distribution, called the sampling distribution."]),
    ("Sampling techniques",
     [r"Simple random sampling gives every member of the population the same chance of selection. When the population consists of distinct layers (for example small and large farms), stratified random sampling is more precise: a sample is drawn from each stratum, and the estimates are combined using the stratum sizes as weights.",
      r"Cluster sampling selects whole groups (for example villages); it is cheap but less precise when members of a cluster are alike. Systematic sampling takes every \(k\)-th member of an ordered list. Non-random samples (convenience or voluntary samples) do not allow a valid calculation of sampling error."]),
    ("Experimental and observational data",
     [r"In an experiment, the researcher assigns treatments and randomises them to experimental units, so differences in the response can be interpreted as caused by the treatments. In an observational study, the researcher only records what happens; a relationship found may be confounded by a variable that affects both the predictor and the response.",
      r"This is why experimental design emphasises randomisation, while regression on observational data needs care: a regression coefficient shows an association after the other variables in the model are controlled for, not automatically a cause-and-effect relationship."]),
    ("Preparing data before analysis",
     [r"Store data in long (tidy) format: one row per observation, one column per variable, and the first row holding the column names. Factor columns are best stored as text (V1, V2) so they are not read as numbers. Check for missing values, typos, mixed units, and impossible values (such as a negative height) before running any test.",
      r"Missing values must never be filled with zero. If there are few and they occur at random, analysing the available data is usually enough; if there are many or they follow a pattern, report it and consider specialised methods. Investigate the cause of an outlier first: a recording error is corrected, while a genuine extreme value is kept and reported."])],
   [("Give the measurement scale of: a player's shirt number, a race ranking (1st, 2nd, 3rd), temperature in °C, and body weight.",
     r"Shirt number: nominal (a label only). Race ranking: ordinal (ordered, unequal gaps). Temperature in °C: interval (arbitrary zero). Body weight: ratio (true zero)."),
    ("A population of 600 farmers consists of 360 small-farm and 240 large-farm farmers. With a proportional sample of 50 farmers, how many come from each stratum? If the stratum mean yields are 4.2 and 6.0 tonnes, what is the estimated population mean?",
     r"Allocation: \(50\times 360/600=30\) and \(50\times 240/600=20\) farmers. Estimate: \(0.6\times 4.2+0.4\times 6.0=2.52+2.40=4.92\) tonnes."),
    ("A survey finds that farmers who use organic fertiliser have higher yields. Can we conclude that organic fertiliser increases yield?",
     r"Not yet. These data are observational; farmers who use organic fertiliser may also differ in capital, land, or experience (confounders). A causal conclusion needs a randomised experiment, or at least control of the confounders in the model.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: estimating from a stratified sample",
                soal="A population of 1,000 orchards is divided into three strata by altitude. A proportional sample of 50 orchards is taken. The mean yield in each stratum (t/ha) is shown in the table. Estimate the population mean.",
                data=[["Stratum", "N_h", "W_h", "n_h", "Mean"], ["Lowland", "500", "0.5", "25", "12"], ["Midland", "300", "0.3", "15", "15"], ["Highland", "200", "0.2", "10", "20"]],
                langkah=["The stratum weight is its share of the population.",
                         "Proportional allocation divides the 50 sampled units according to those weights.",
                         "The stratified mean is the weighted average of the stratum means."],
                simpul="The estimated population mean is 14.5 t/ha. A simple unweighted average (15.67) would overestimate it because it gives too much weight to the small highland stratum."))

LE("dasar-statistik-deskriptif",
   [("Quartiles, percentiles, and the box plot",
     [r"The \(p\)-th percentile is the value below which \(p\%\) of the data lie. The first quartile \(Q_1\), the median \(Q_2\), and the third quartile \(Q_3\) are the 25th, 50th, and 75th percentiles. The five-number summary (minimum, \(Q_1\), median, \(Q_3\), maximum) is drawn as a box plot.",
      r"Tukey's rule flags an observation as an outlier when it lies more than 1.5 times the IQR below \(Q_1\) or above \(Q_3\). The rule only flags values for checking; it is not an automatic reason to delete data. Note that software uses several interpolation methods for quartiles, so values can differ slightly in small samples."]),
    ("Computational formula for the variance",
     [r"The sum of squared deviations can be computed without first computing each deviation, using only the sum of the values and the sum of their squares. This form is used for hand calculation and is also the basis of the correction factor (CF) in an analysis of variance table.",
      r"For computer calculation with large numbers, the two-pass formula (compute the mean first) is numerically more stable, because the shortcut formula subtracts two large, nearly equal numbers."]),
    ("Grouped data in a frequency table",
     [r"When data are available only as a frequency table, each class is represented by its midpoint \(m_k\) with frequency \(f_k\). The mean and variance are then computed as weighted averages. The result is an approximation, because the original values within a class are assumed to sit at the midpoint.",
      r"The median of grouped data is found by linear interpolation within the median class, the class where the cumulative frequency passes \(n/2\)."]),
    ("Presenting summaries by group",
     [r"In an experimental report, the mean of each treatment is usually presented with a measure of spread. Use the standard deviation (mean ± SD) to describe variation among individuals, and the standard error (mean ± SE) to describe the precision of the mean. Always state which one is used, together with the sample size.",
      r"Match the number of decimals to the precision of the instrument; usually one more decimal than the raw data. For strongly skewed data, the median and IQR are more honest than the mean and standard deviation."])],
   [(r"Data: 4, 7, 7, 8, 10, 25. Compute the mean and the median. Which better represents the centre of the data?",
     r"Mean \(=61/6=10.17\); median \(=(7+8)/2=7.5\). The median is more representative because the value 25 pulls the mean upward."),
    (r"Given \(n=5\), \(\sum x=50\), and \(\sum x^2=530\). Compute the variance and standard deviation with the shortcut formula.",
     r"\(\sum(x-\bar{x})^2=530-50^2/5=530-500=30\). Variance \(s^2=30/4=7.5\) and \(s=\sqrt{7.5}=2.74\)."),
    (r"A data set has \(Q_1=12\) and \(Q_3=18\). Is the value 30 an outlier by Tukey's rule?",
     r"\(IQR=6\), upper fence \(=18+1.5(6)=27\). The value 30 exceeds the upper fence, so it is flagged as an outlier and its cause should be checked.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: grouped data",
                soal="The weights of 20 fruits (grams) are available only in the frequency table below. Compute the approximate mean, variance, and standard deviation.",
                data=[["Class", "Midpoint m", "Frequency f", "f·m", "f·m²"], ["10–14", "12", "3", "36", "432"], ["15–19", "17", "7", "119", "2,023"], ["20–24", "22", "6", "132", "2,904"], ["25–29", "27", "4", "108", "2,916"], ["Total", "", "20", "395", "8,275"]],
                langkah=["Weighted mean of the midpoints.",
                         "Sum of squared deviations with the shortcut formula.",
                         "Variance and standard deviation with divisor n − 1 = 19."],
                simpul="The mean weight is about 19.75 g with a standard deviation of about 4.99 g. Because the original values were replaced by midpoints, these figures are approximations; if raw data are available, compute directly from them."))

LE("dasar-distribusi-normal",
   [("Sampling distribution of the mean",
     [r"A linear combination of independent normal variables is also normal. As a result, the mean of \(n\) observations from a normal population is normal with mean \(\mu\) and variance \(\sigma^2/n\). This is the basis of the standard error: the larger \(n\), the narrower the distribution of \(\bar{X}\) around \(\mu\).",
      r"If the population is not normal, the central limit theorem guarantees the same result approximately for large enough \(n\). How large is “large enough” depends on the skewness of the population: for a nearly symmetric distribution \(n\approx 15\) is adequate, while a strongly skewed distribution needs more."]),
    ("Derived distributions: chi-square, t, and F",
     [r"Three distributions used in nearly every test come from the normal distribution. The sum of squares of \(k\) standard normal variables follows a chi-square distribution with \(k\) degrees of freedom. A standard normal divided by the square root of a chi-square per degree of freedom follows a t distribution. The ratio of two chi-squares, each divided by its degrees of freedom, follows an F distribution.",
      r"These give the familiar tests: the t statistic for a mean (because \(\sigma\) is estimated by \(s\)), the chi-square test for a variance, and the F test in the analysis of variance as the ratio of two mean squares."]),
    ("Normal approximation to the binomial and Poisson",
     [r"A binomial distribution with large \(n\) can be approximated by a normal when \(np\) and \(n(1-p)\) are both at least about 10. Because the binomial is discrete, add a continuity correction of 0.5: the probability \(X\le k\) is approximated by the normal area up to \(k+0.5\). A Poisson distribution with large \(\lambda\) can also be approximated by a normal with mean and variance \(\lambda\).",
      r"This approximation is used in tests of proportions and explains why count data with a large mean often look nearly normal."]),
    ("Transformations towards normality",
     [r"When data are clearly not normal, a transformation often improves the shape of the distribution and stabilises the variance at the same time. The logarithm suits positive, right-skewed data or a variance proportional to the square of the mean; the square root suits counts; the arcsine square root suits proportions. The Box-Cox transformation chooses the power \(\lambda\) that brings the data closest to normal.",
      r"After analysing on the transformed scale, report results on the original scale with the back-transformation; remember that a back-transformed mean of logarithms is the geometric mean, not the arithmetic mean."])],
   [(r"Plant height follows \(N(70,\ 8^2)\) cm. What is the probability that one plant is taller than 82 cm?",
     r"\(z=(82-70)/8=1.5\). \(P(Z>1.5)=0.0668\), about 6.7%."),
    (r"From the same population, 16 plants are sampled. What is the probability that their sample mean exceeds 74 cm?",
     r"\(SE=8/\sqrt{16}=2\), \(z=(74-70)/2=2\). \(P(Z>2)=0.0228\), about 2.3%. The probability is much smaller than for a single plant because the mean varies less."),
    (r"What height marks the tallest 10% of plants?",
     r"The 90th percentile of the standard normal is \(z_{0.90}=1.2816\). The cut-off is \(70+1.2816\times 8=80.25\) cm.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: normal approximation to the binomial",
                soal="Seed germination is 30%. Of 100 seeds sown, what is the probability that at most 25 germinate?",
                data=None,
                langkah=["Check the conditions for the approximation: np and n(1 − p) are large enough.",
                         "Mean and standard deviation of the binomial distribution.",
                         "Use the continuity correction: X ≤ 25 is approximated by the normal area up to 25.5.",
                         "Area below z from the standard normal table."],
                simpul="The probability is about 16.3%, very close to the exact binomial probability (0.1631). Without the continuity correction the result is 0.1376, much further off."))

LE("dasar-uji-hipotesis",
   [("Two-sample tests: independent and paired",
     [r"For two independent groups, Welch's t test compares means without assuming equal variances; its degrees of freedom are estimated with the Welch–Satterthwaite formula. The pooled t test is valid only when both groups have the same variance.",
      r"When observations are paired (before and after on the same subject, or two treatments on twin plots), the analysis is done on the difference within each pair. Pairing removes the between-subject variation, so the paired test is usually much more powerful than treating the data as two independent groups."]),
    ("One-sided and two-sided tests",
     [r"A two-sided alternative (\(\mu\neq\mu_0\)) is used when a difference in either direction matters. A one-sided alternative (\(\mu>\mu_0\)) is valid only if the direction is fixed before seeing the data and a difference in the other direction is irrelevant to the decision. The one-sided P-value is half the two-sided P-value when the statistic points in the hypothesised direction.",
      r"Choosing the direction after seeing the data silently doubles the type I error; that is why scientific reports generally use two-sided tests."]),
    ("Test for a proportion",
     [r"For binary data (success or failure), the sample proportion \(\hat{p}=x/n\) is compared with a hypothesised value \(p_0\). When \(np_0\) and \(n(1-p_0)\) are large enough, the z statistic is approximately standard normal. For small samples use the exact binomial test."]),
    ("Power and sample size",
     [r"Power is the probability of rejecting \(H_0\) when the true effect has size \(\delta\). Power increases when \(\delta\) is large, \(\sigma\) is small, \(n\) is large, or \(\alpha\) is less strict. Before the experiment, decide the smallest practically meaningful effect, an estimate of \(\sigma\) (from the literature or a pilot study), \(\alpha\), and the target power (usually 0.8 or 0.9), then compute \(n\).",
      r"The formula below uses the normal approximation; for small samples it is slightly low and one or two observations are usually added."]),
    ("Companion nonparametric tests",
     [r"When data are clearly non-normal and the sample is small, rank-based tests are an alternative: the Wilcoxon signed-rank test for paired or one-sample data, the Mann–Whitney test (Wilcoxon rank-sum) for two independent groups, and Kruskal–Wallis for more than two groups. These tests detect a shift in the location of the distribution rather than a difference in means specifically, and are slightly less powerful when the data are actually normal."])],
   [(r"A sample of 25 plots gives \(\bar{x}=52\) and \(s=5\). Test \(H_0:\mu=50\) two-sided at \(\alpha=0.05\).",
     r"\(t=(52-50)/(5/\sqrt{25})=2.00\), \(df=24\), two-sided P \(=0.057\). \(H_0\) is not rejected at 5% (although a one-sided upward test gives \(P=0.028\), that direction must be fixed before seeing the data)."),
    (r"What sample size is needed for one group to detect a difference of 5 units with 90% power at \(\alpha=0.05\) two-sided, if \(\sigma=10\)?",
     r"\(n=\big((1.96+1.2816)\times 10/5\big)^2=42.0\). Round up: 43 observations."),
    (r"Of 100 respondents, 60 agree. Test whether the proportion who agree differs from 0.5.",
     r"\(z=(0.6-0.5)/\sqrt{0.25/100}=2.00\), two-sided P \(=0.0455\). At \(\alpha=0.05\), \(H_0\) is rejected: the proportion who agree differs from one half.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: paired t test",
                soal="The chlorophyll content of six plants was measured before and after a foliar fertiliser. The differences (after − before) are shown in the table. Does the fertiliser increase chlorophyll content?",
                data=[["Plant", "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6"], ["Difference d", "2.1", "1.4", "3.0", "0.8", "1.9", "2.4"]],
                langkah=["Mean and standard deviation of the differences.",
                         "Standard error of the mean difference.",
                         "The t statistic with df = 5.",
                         "Compare with the critical value or compute the two-sided P-value."],
                simpul="The mean increase of 1.93 units is highly significant (P = 0.0016). Because each plant serves as its own control, the variation between plants does not enter the error."))

LE("dasar-selang-kepercayaan",
   [("The correct interpretation",
     [r"A 95% confidence interval means: if sampling were repeated many times and an interval built the same way each time, about 95% of those intervals would contain the true parameter. For one interval already computed, the parameter is fixed (not random); it is the interval that is random.",
      r"So the sentence “the probability that \(\mu\) lies between 17.9 and 22.1 is 95%” is not quite right in the frequentist framework. A safe sentence is: “we are 95% confident that the interval 17.9 to 22.1 contains \(\mu\)”, where confidence refers to the procedure."]),
    ("Confidence interval for a proportion",
     [r"The Wald interval \(\hat{p}\pm z\sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})/n}\) is easy, but its coverage is often below nominal when \(n\) is small or \(\hat{p}\) is near 0 or 1, and it can cross the 0–1 bounds. The Wilson (score) interval is much better and is recommended as the default; a simple alternative that is almost as good is Agresti–Coull, which adds two successes and two failures before applying the Wald formula."]),
    ("Confidence interval for a variance",
     [r"Because \((n-1)s^2/\sigma^2\) follows a chi-square distribution, an interval for \(\sigma^2\) is obtained by inverting chi-square quantiles. This interval is not symmetric around \(s^2\) and is very sensitive to non-normality, so use it with care."]),
    ("Link with hypothesis tests",
     [r"A two-sided \(100(1-\alpha)\%\) confidence interval contains exactly the values \(\mu_0\) that a two-sided test at level \(\alpha\) does not reject. So if the interval for a difference between two means contains zero, the difference is not significant at that level. An interval is more informative than a P-value because it shows both the size and the precision of the effect."]),
    ("Bootstrap intervals",
     [r"When no formula is available or its assumptions are doubtful (for example for a median or a ratio), an interval can be built by the bootstrap: resample \(n\) observations with replacement from the data, compute the statistic, repeat thousands of times, then take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the results as a 95% interval (percentile method)."])],
   [(r"A sample of \(n=16\) gives \(\bar{x}=20\) and \(s=4\). Build a 95% confidence interval for \(\mu\).",
     r"\(SE=4/4=1\), \(t_{0.025;15}=2.131\). Interval: \(20\pm2.131\), that is 17.87 to 22.13."),
    (r"The 95% confidence interval for the difference in mean yield between two fertilisers is −0.4 to 3.1 t/ha. What do you conclude?",
     r"The interval contains zero, so the difference is not significant at \(\alpha=0.05\). But the interval also shows that a difference of up to 3.1 t/ha is still plausible; the data are not precise enough to conclude that there is no difference."),
    (r"A sample of \(n=10\) gives \(s^2=9\). Build a 95% confidence interval for \(\sigma^2\).",
     r"\(\chi^2_{0.975;9}=19.023\) and \(\chi^2_{0.025;9}=2.700\). Interval: \(81/19.023=4.26\) to \(81/2.700=30.0\).")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: Wald and Wilson intervals for a proportion",
                soal="Of 60 seedlings, 18 are diseased. Build 95% confidence intervals for the proportion diseased using the Wald and Wilson methods.",
                data=None,
                langkah=["Sample proportion and its standard error.",
                         "Wald interval.",
                         "The Wilson centre moves slightly towards 0.5.",
                         "Half-width of the Wilson interval.",
                         "Wilson interval."],
                simpul="With n = 60 the two intervals are similar; Wilson shifts slightly towards 0.5 and its coverage is more reliable. The difference grows when n is small or the proportion is near 0 or 1."))

LE("dasar-asumsi-residual",
   [("Checking normality: plots and formal tests",
     [r"The normality to check is that of the residuals, not of each raw data column. A quantile-quantile (Q-Q) plot places the ordered residuals against normal quantiles; points following a straight line indicate normality, while curved ends indicate heavy tails or skewness.",
      r"The Shapiro–Wilk test is the most powerful normality test for small to moderate samples. The Kolmogorov–Smirnov test with the Lilliefors correction is also common. In large samples formal tests readily reject small, unimportant departures, while in small samples they easily miss large ones; so read the test together with the plot."]),
    ("Tests of equal variance: Bartlett and Levene",
     [r"Bartlett's test compares group variances with a chi-square statistic, but it is very sensitive to non-normality. Levene's test is more robust: it runs a one-way ANOVA on the absolute deviation of each observation from its group mean. The Brown–Forsythe version uses the group median and is even more robust to skewed data.",
      r"Practical guide: when the ratio of the largest to the smallest variance is below about 3 and group sizes are balanced, ANOVA is fairly robust to unequal variances."]),
    ("The Durbin–Watson statistic",
     [r"For data ordered in time or space, the Durbin–Watson statistic tests first-order autocorrelation in the residuals. Its value is close to \(2(1-\hat{\rho})\): around 2 means no autocorrelation, near 0 means positive autocorrelation, and near 4 means negative autocorrelation.",
      r"The decision uses the bounds \(d_L\) and \(d_U\) from tables by \(n\) and the number of predictors: \(d<d_L\) means significant positive autocorrelation, \(d>d_U\) means not significant, and in between the test is inconclusive."]),
    ("Choosing a transformation with Box-Cox",
     [r"Box-Cox searches for the power \(\lambda\) that maximises the likelihood of the model after transforming the response. In practice \(\lambda\) is rounded to an interpretable value: 1 (no transformation), 0.5 (square root), 0 (logarithm), −1 (reciprocal). If the confidence interval for \(\lambda\) contains 1, no transformation is needed.",
      r"The mean–variance relationship can also guide the choice: if the group standard deviations are proportional to the means, use the logarithm; if the variances are proportional to the means (counts), use the square root."]),
    ("Additivity: Tukey's one-degree-of-freedom test",
     [r"In a blocked design without replication within cells, the model assumes that block and treatment effects are additive. Tukey's test for nonadditivity separates one degree of freedom from the error to detect a multiplicative interaction. If it is significant, a log transformation often restores additivity."])],
   [(r"A regression with \(n=30\) and one predictor gives \(d=0.90\). From the table, \(d_L=1.35\) and \(d_U=1.49\). What do you conclude?",
     r"\(d=0.90<d_L=1.35\), so there is significant positive autocorrelation. Ordinary regression standard errors are too small; consider a time-series model or autocorrelation-robust standard errors."),
    (r"Insect count data show a variance that rises with the group mean. What is an appropriate action?",
     r"A square-root transformation \(\sqrt{y+0.5}\) to stabilise the variance, or better, model the counts directly with Poisson or negative binomial regression."),
    (r"Box-Cox gives \(\hat{\lambda}=0.08\) with a 95% interval of −0.20 to 0.35. Which transformation should be chosen?",
     r"The interval contains 0 but not 1, so choose the logarithm \(\ln y\).")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: the Durbin–Watson statistic",
                soal="Eight time-ordered residuals from a regression are shown in the table. Compute the Durbin–Watson statistic and the estimated autocorrelation.",
                data=[["t", "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7", "8"], ["e_t", "0.5", "0.8", "0.3", "−0.2", "−0.6", "−0.4", "0.1", "0.4"]],
                langkah=["Sum of squared differences between consecutive residuals.",
                         "Sum of squared residuals.",
                         "The d statistic and the estimated autocorrelation."],
                simpul="The value d = 0.66 is far below 2: neighbouring residuals tend to have the same sign (positive autocorrelation). For time-series data, the assumption of independent errors is not met."))

LE("dasar-prinsip-rancangan",
   [("Experimental units and pseudoreplication",
     [r"The experimental unit is the smallest unit that receives a treatment independently through randomisation, such as one pot, one plot, or one pen. The observational unit is where measurements are taken, such as each plant in a pot. Replication is counted by the number of experimental units, not the number of measurements.",
      r"Treating multiple measurements within one experimental unit as replicates is called pseudoreplication. The error degrees of freedom become too large, the standard errors too small, and the P-values too optimistic. The fix: average the observations per experimental unit, or model them as a nested design."]),
    ("Treatment structure and design structure",
     [r"Every experiment has two separate structures. The treatment structure describes which treatments are compared: one factor, factorial, or factorial plus a control. The design structure describes how treatments are randomised to experimental units: CRD, RCBD, Latin square, or split plot.",
      r"The two are combined in one model; for example “a 3×2 factorial in an RCBD” means a factorial treatment structure and a randomised complete block design structure. Separating them makes it easier to choose the right analysis."]),
    ("Controls, placebos, and blinding",
     [r"A control treatment is the benchmark for comparison: a negative control (no treatment), a positive control (a standard treatment known to work), or a placebo in experiments involving people. Blinding prevents the observer or subject from knowing the treatment so that assessment is not biased, especially for subjective responses such as damage scores or taste tests."]),
    ("Number of replicates from power",
     [r"The number of replicates should be set from power, not habit. To compare two treatments with a smallest detectable difference \(\delta\) and error standard deviation \(\sigma\), the normal approximation gives the replicates per treatment. For \(t\) treatments in an ANOVA, power is computed from the noncentral F distribution with noncentrality parameter \(\lambda\).",
      r"An old rule of thumb in field experiments is at least about 12–15 error degrees of freedom, because below that the critical values of F and t rise sharply and the test loses power."]),
    ("Carrying out the randomisation",
     [r"In a CRD, all \(t\times r\) experimental units are numbered and treatments are allocated by a sequence of random numbers. In an RCBD, randomisation is done separately within each block so that each treatment appears once per block. Record the randomisation (field layout) before the experiment starts, and do not change it after seeing field conditions."])],
   [(r"Four pots per treatment, and in each pot five plants are measured for height. How many replicates per treatment are there?",
     r"Four, because the pot is the experimental unit that receives the treatment. The five plants per pot are subsamples; treating 20 plants as replicates is pseudoreplication."),
    (r"How many replicates per treatment are needed to detect a difference of 2 units between two treatments with 80% power at \(\alpha=0.05\), if \(\sigma=3\)?",
     r"\(r\approx2(1.96+0.8416)^2(9)/4=35.3\). Round up: 36 replicates per treatment."),
    (r"The experimental field slopes from north to south. How should blocks be laid out in an RCBD?",
     r"Blocks run perpendicular to the slope (west–east), so that each block lies at nearly the same elevation and the variation due to the slope goes into the block component rather than the error.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: choosing the number of replicates",
                soal="A researcher wants to compare two varieties. From earlier experiments, the error standard deviation is about 4 quintals/ha. The smallest agronomically meaningful difference is 3 quintals/ha. How many replicates are needed for 80% power at α = 0.05 two-sided?",
                data=None,
                langkah=["Normal quantiles for α/2 = 0.025 and power 0.80.",
                         "Substitute into the formula for replicates per treatment.",
                         "Round up."],
                simpul="About 28 replicates per variety are needed. If that is not feasible, the options are: accept lower power, reduce σ with blocking or more uniform experimental units, or target a larger difference."))

LE("dasar-anova",
   [("Assumptions and diagnostics",
     [r"The F test in ANOVA is valid when the errors are independent, normally distributed, and have the same variance in all treatments. Independence is ensured mainly by randomisation. Normality is checked on the residuals \(e_{ij}=y_{ij}-\bar{y}_{i\cdot}\) with a Q-Q plot or Shapiro–Wilk. Equal variance is checked with Levene's test and a plot of residuals against fitted values.",
      r"ANOVA is fairly robust to mild non-normality, especially with balanced replication. The most damaging violations are lack of independence (such as pseudoreplication) and very different variances combined with unequal group sizes."]),
    ("Effect size: eta-squared and omega-squared",
     [r"The P-value does not show how large the treatment effect is. Eta-squared \(\eta^2\) is the proportion of total variation explained by treatments, but it tends to overestimate in small samples. Omega-squared \(\omega^2\) corrects that bias and better describes the effect in the population.",
      r"Cohen gives rough benchmarks for ANOVA: about 0.01 small, 0.06 medium, and 0.14 large. Field-specific benchmarks are more meaningful when available."]),
    ("Orthogonal contrasts",
     [r"A contrast is a linear combination of treatment means whose coefficients sum to zero, such as comparing a control with the mean of two other treatments. Contrasts planned before the experiment are tested directly with a one-degree-of-freedom F, without waiting for the overall F to be significant.",
      r"Two contrasts are orthogonal when the sum of the products of their coefficients is zero. A set of \(t-1\) mutually orthogonal contrasts splits the treatment sum of squares into parts that add up exactly to SSTR. For equally spaced quantitative treatments (for example doses 0, 50, 100), orthogonal polynomial contrasts test linear, quadratic, and higher trends."]),
    ("When the assumptions fail",
     [r"When variances are unequal, Welch's ANOVA compares means without assuming equal variances, using weights \(w_i=n_i/s_i^2\). When residuals are clearly non-normal and replication is small, the rank-based Kruskal–Wallis test is a nonparametric alternative. Transforming the response (logarithm, square root) often solves both problems at once.",
      r"Whichever option you choose, base it on diagnostics and the nature of the data, not on trying several tests and picking the one with the smallest P."])],
   [(r"An experiment with 4 treatments and 5 replicates gives SSTR = 60 and SSTO = 140. Complete the ANOVA table and draw a conclusion at \(\alpha=0.05\).",
     r"SSE \(=140-60=80\); treatment df 3, error df 16. MSTR \(=20\), MSE \(=5\), \(F=4.00\). \(F_{0.05;3,16}=3.24\), P \(=0.027\). Treatments have a significant effect."),
    (r"Three treatment means are 10 (control), 12, and 15, with \(r=6\) and MSE \(=4\). Test the contrast of the control against the mean of the other two treatments.",
     r"Coefficients \((2,-1,-1)\): \(L=20-27=-7\). \(SS_L=49/(6/6)=49\), \(F=49/4=12.25\) with df 1 and 15, P \(=0.003\). The control is significantly lower than the mean of the other treatments."),
    (r"Why is \(\eta^2\) often larger than \(\omega^2\)?",
     r"\(\eta^2\) uses SSTR as it is, although SSTR also contains random error variation of roughly \((t-1)MSE\). \(\omega^2\) subtracts that part, so it describes the population effect more honestly, especially in small samples.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: splitting the treatment SS with orthogonal contrasts",
                soal="Continuing the three-feed example (A, B, C) with four replicates: means 6, 8, and 10 kg, SSTR = 32, and MSE = 0.667 (error df 9). A is the standard feed. Split SSTR into two orthogonal contrasts: A versus the mean of (B, C), and B versus C.",
                data=[["Contrast", "c_A", "c_B", "c_C", "L", "SS"], ["A versus (B, C)", "2", "−1", "−1", "−6", "24"], ["B versus C", "0", "1", "−1", "−2", "8"]],
                langkah=["Check orthogonality: the sum of products of the coefficients is zero.",
                         "The first contrast and its sum of squares.",
                         "The second contrast and its sum of squares.",
                         "Together they equal the treatment sum of squares.",
                         "F test for each contrast with df 1 and 9."],
                simpul="Both contrasts are significant: the standard feed A is lower than the mean of the new feeds, and C is better than B. The overall effect size is large: η² = 32/38 = 0.84 and ω² = 0.79."))

LE("dasar-uji-lanjut",
   [("Critical-value formulas",
     [r"Every pairwise post-hoc test compares the difference between two means with a critical value built from a standard error. With equal replication \(r\), the standard error of a difference is \(\sqrt{2MSE/r}\). The tests differ in the distribution used: LSD uses t, HSD (Tukey) uses the studentised range \(q\), Duncan uses \(q\) with a level that changes with the rank distance, and Scheffé uses F so it is valid for all contrasts.",
      r"With unequal replication, replace \(2/r\) by \(1/r_i+1/r_j\) (Tukey–Kramer for HSD)."]),
    ("Per-comparison and family-wise error rates",
     [r"If every comparison is tested at \(\alpha\), the probability of at least one false conclusion in an experiment (the family-wise error rate, FWER) grows with the number of comparisons. With \(t\) treatments there are \(t(t-1)/2\) pairs; six treatments already give 15 pairs.",
      r"The Bonferroni correction tests each comparison at \(\alpha/m\); simple but conservative. Holm's procedure is more powerful and still controls the FWER: order the P-values from smallest, compare the \(k\)-th P with \(\alpha/(m-k+1)\), and stop at the first failure."]),
    ("Post-hoc tests when variances are unequal",
     [r"LSD, HSD, and Duncan use a single pooled MSE and so assume all treatment variances are equal. When variances clearly differ, use Games–Howell, a version of Tukey that uses Welch's standard error and degrees of freedom for each pair."]),
    ("Choosing a post-hoc test",
     [r"Choose the test before seeing the data, according to the research question. For a few planned comparisons, LSD or contrasts are enough. For comparing all pairs, Tukey (HSD) controls the FWER and is a safe default. For comparing each treatment with a single control, Dunnett is more powerful than Tukey. For quantitative treatments (doses, temperatures), do not use pairwise tests; use polynomial contrasts or regression.",
      r"Duncan (DMRT) is widely used in the agricultural literature because it finds differences more easily, but its FWER is looser than Tukey's; state clearly which test was used."])],
   [(r"MSE = 4 with 16 error df, and each treatment has 5 replicates. Compute the 5% LSD.",
     r"\(t_{0.025;16}=2.120\). \(LSD=2.120\sqrt{2(4)/5}=2.120(1.265)=2.68\). Two means differ significantly if their difference exceeds 2.68."),
    (r"With the same data and 4 treatments, compute the 5% HSD (Tukey) if \(q_{0.05;4,16}=4.05\).",
     r"\(HSD=4.05\sqrt{4/5}=4.05(0.894)=3.62\). HSD is larger than LSD because it controls the error for all 6 pairs at once."),
    (r"If six independent comparisons are each tested at \(\alpha=0.05\), what is the probability of at least one false positive?",
     r"\(1-0.95^6=0.265\), about 26.5%, far above 5%.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: Holm correction for four comparisons",
                soal="Four planned comparisons give P-values 0.004, 0.019, 0.030, and 0.21. Apply the Bonferroni and Holm corrections at a 5% FWER.",
                data=[["Rank k", "P", "Bonferroni (P × 4)", "Holm multiplier", "Holm P (adjusted)"], ["1", "0.004", "0.016", "4", "0.016"], ["2", "0.019", "0.076", "3", "0.057"], ["3", "0.030", "0.120", "2", "0.060"], ["4", "0.210", "0.840", "1", "0.210"]],
                langkah=["Bonferroni: multiply each P by m = 4 (maximum 1).",
                         "Holm: multiply the k-th ordered P by m − k + 1.",
                         "Keep the adjusted P from decreasing (take the cumulative maximum), then compare with 0.05."],
                simpul="At a 5% FWER, only the comparison with P = 0.004 remains significant. Without correction, three comparisons look significant; two of them could well be false positives."))

LE("dasar-korelasi",
   [("Coefficient of determination and the size of a correlation",
     [r"The squared correlation \(r^2\) is the proportion of the variation in one variable explained by its linear relationship with the other. A correlation of 0.5 explains only 25% of the variation. Cohen gives rough benchmarks for \(|r|\): 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, and 0.5 large; field-specific benchmarks are more meaningful.",
      r"Whether a correlation is significant depends heavily on \(n\): with \(n=400\), a correlation of 0.1 is already significant although the relationship is almost negligible. So report its confidence interval, not just the P-value."]),
    ("Confidence interval with Fisher's transformation",
     [r"The distribution of \(r\) is skewed when \(\rho\) is far from zero, so the interval is built on Fisher's scale \(z=\tanh^{-1}(r)\), which is approximately normal with standard error \(1/\sqrt{n-3}\). The limits are returned to the correlation scale with \(\tanh\). The same transformation is used to compare two correlations from independent samples."]),
    ("Partial correlation",
     [r"Two variables can be correlated only because both are influenced by a third variable. The partial correlation measures the relationship between \(X\) and \(Y\) after removing the linear effect of \(Z\) from both. If the partial correlation is much smaller than the ordinary correlation, most of the original relationship was due to \(Z\)."]),
    ("Kendall's tau",
     [r"Besides Spearman, Kendall's rank correlation \(\tau\) measures agreement in ordering from the number of concordant pairs minus the number of discordant pairs. It is generally smaller than Spearman's for the same data, more robust to outliers, and has a clear probability interpretation."]),
    ("Limits of correlation",
     [r"Pearson's correlation measures only linear relationships. A U-shaped relationship can give \(r\approx0\) even though the variables are clearly related. Anscombe's four data sets have nearly identical means, variances, and correlations, yet very different scatter plots; so always draw a scatter plot.",
      r"A single outlier can raise or lower \(r\) dramatically, and a restricted range of data lowers the correlation. Most importantly: correlation does not prove causation."])],
   [(r"From 20 pairs of data, \(r=0.45\). Is the correlation significant at \(\alpha=0.05\)?",
     r"\(t=0.45\sqrt{18}/\sqrt{1-0.2025}=2.14\) with df 18, P \(=0.046\). The correlation is significant but explains only \(r^2=20\%\) of the variation."),
    (r"The correlation between rainfall and yield is 0.6. What percentage of the variation in yield can be explained linearly by rainfall?",
     r"\(r^2=0.36\), that is 36%."),
    (r"A scatter plot shows a clear U shape, but \(r=0.02\). What do you conclude?",
     r"There is no linear relationship, but there is a strong nonlinear one. Use a model that matches the shape, such as quadratic polynomial regression.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: confidence interval for a correlation",
                soal="Across 30 plots, the correlation between leaf nitrogen and yield is r = 0.62. Build a 95% confidence interval for ρ and test whether the correlation is significant.",
                data=None,
                langkah=["t test for the correlation with df = 28.",
                         "Fisher's transformation and its standard error.",
                         "95% interval on the z scale.",
                         "Return to the correlation scale."],
                simpul="The correlation is highly significant, and the population ρ is estimated to lie between 0.33 and 0.80. The interval is not symmetric around 0.62 and is fairly wide, so the true strength of the relationship is still uncertain with 30 plots."))
